#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074874} :activity/id #uuid "ec7b59c9-abe8-57a3-a286-1bbf406859b0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050174 :topic/id #uuid "33fcd530-1cfd-5f42-a911-762522fe7130" :topic/math-academy-id 563} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074874 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074967 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we are given a ratio and want to find an amount. In these cases, it's helpful to use equivalent fractions. \n\nFor example, if the ratio of cows to sheep on Margaret's farm is $11:7$, and she has $22$ cows, how many sheep are there on the farm?\n\nTo solve this, we start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}11}:{\\color{red}7}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}11}{\\color{red}7}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of cows and the denominator represents the number of sheep.\n\nSince there are $22$ cows on the farm, we would like a numerator of $22.$ We make this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}11}\\times 2}{{\\color{red}7}\\times 2} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}22}}{{\\color{red}14}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is now the number of sheep on the farm if there are $22$ cows. Therefore, on Margaret's farm, there are $\\color{red}14$ sheep." :tutorial/id #uuid "cfd89526-b68e-5436-a4e1-e20aa477ab68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2050 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1f40eb50-1f7f-5b78-a04c-ac11239e4005" :step/math-academy-id 7792 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074875}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074875 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073881 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073563 :question/id #uuid "913c1995-fe26-5359-8298-e3b8379e39d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-2277"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48f71a07-2bd3-5164-a227-b82d56c0abc7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050481}] 